        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> The problem is that colemak's i is qwerty's l, and I need to keep the neovim convention, so I need to map i to l.\r\n> At the same time, I needed a key instead of i, for entering insert mode and text objects, and I chose the k key.\r\n\r\nI am not sure that manually choosing what keys to remap is a good idea here. I would assume that the better approach is to remap the whole keyboard layout altogether. For example, judging by [Wikipedia article](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colemak), a \"key instead if i\" is \"u\".\r\n\r\nHere is an [article with 'langmap'](https://www.xudongz.com/blog/2017/vim-colemak/) example. With those remappings, pressing \"u\" on Colemak will have the same effect as \"i\" in QWERTY. So, for example, to delete inside word (\"diw\" in QWERTY) press \"suw\".\r\n\r\nThis is the only way 'mini.ai' will support different keyboard layouts (outside of already exported `config.mappings`).",
