        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> The problem is that colemak's i is qwerty's l, and I need to keep the neovim convention, so I need to map i to l. At the same time, I needed a key instead of i, for entering insert mode and text objects, and I chose the k key. The current mapping situation is as follows:\r\n> \r\n> ```\r\n> vim.keymap.set({ \"n\", \"x\" }, \"i\", \"l\")\r\n> vim.keymap.set({ \"n\", \"x\", \"o\" }, \"k\", \"i\")\r\n> ```\r\n> \r\n> So my scenario involves three keys, i, l, k, and I cannot simply swap k and i. The implementation principle of mini.ai is to remap inside, which will theoretically conflict with my keys. Maybe only compatible from within.\r\n\r\n```\r\ncustom_textobjects = {\r\n    w = { \"()()%f[%w]%w+()[ \\t]*()\" },\r\n},\r\n``` \r\nthis might help, only `kw` overwrite keymap ",
